# MANDATORY: TRANSOVO Privacy, Consent & Compliance UX Standards (Guide #8)

**Scope:** All repository work — pages, APIs, `src/lib/`, jobs, and any customer-facing behavior. Read the full source doc before implementing.

**Source of truth:** `docs/TRANSOVO-PRIVACY-CONSENT-COMPLIANCE-STANDARDS.md`

## Hard requirement

Any **UI, API, webhook, job, or data model** involving consent, opt-in/out, SMS permissions, data collection, or compliance **MUST** read and follow this guide in full.

## Non-negotiables

### UI
- **Purpose-specific, channel-specific** consent — not one generic checkbox for everything.
- **Never infer consent** from silence, preselected boxes, or broad Terms acceptance alone.
- **Affirmative action required** where law/carrier policy requires it.
- **STOP/START/HELP** and opt-out flows must be discoverable and honored in UI copy.
- **A2P approval ≠ recipient consent** — never conflate in UI messaging.
- Consent UI must match **what was submitted to carriers/providers**.
- Preserve consent **provenance, scope, timestamp, version** in UX that displays audit/history.
- Privacy settings actually **control runtime behavior**.

### Backend / API / jobs (`src/lib/mctb/`, `src/lib/messaging/`, consent tables, SMS send gates)
- **Enforce consent and suppression at send time** — UI checkbox alone is not enough.
- Record **consent events** with method, scope, timestamp, and identity metadata before outbound SMS.
- Honor **opt-out / STOP** in message pipelines and workers — not only in inbox UI.
- Separate **sender registration (A2P)** from **recipient permission** in code paths and logs.
- Data retention and deletion settings in UI must match **actual storage and purge behavior**.

## On conflict

This guide wins for consent/compliance UX over general form patterns.
